  "final": "I added `clamp(value, lower, upper)` to `src/numbers.py` and extended `checks/public.py`. `python3 checks/public.py` passes.\n\n**Changes**\n- `src/numbers.py`: `add` is unchanged. `clamp` returns `lower` if `value < lower`, `upper` if `value > upper`, and `value` otherwise, so both bounds are inclusive. It raises `ValueError` when `lower > upper`. It uses comparisons only, so it works for any ordered values, not just numbers.\n- `checks/public.py`: kept the original `add(2, 3) == 5` check and added checks for values inside the range, below and above it, at both inclusive bounds, a degenerate range where `lower == upper`, and the `ValueError` for `lower > upper`.\n\nNo files outside `src/` and `checks/` were touched. I did not add a separate test framework; the checks are plain asserts, matching the existing script.",
